5|0@zen^1439075590#ctwbzp1:songhill$:I like what Izakaya had to say. &gt;So, stop the pretense. You let /ewk dominate. You say that he dominates because the users let him. You write treatises for him and about him. You approve his behavior, and chastise his critics. The various moderators here have for years now. You're now having an apparently serious discussion about whether Zen is Buddhism. To be sure, ewk is the dominate player here. It is smash mouth Zen all the way. Sincere discussions about various Zen topics are quickly brought to heel when ewk steps in. It's AMA, OP that up, not Zen, claim, prove it, coward, etc. Such responses are intended to stifle conversations; not encourage them. What I find interesting about ewk is his book *Not Zen* and how its ideology works its way into the forum's dynamics. It is a book written by a newbie for newbies. When he says in his book, "I am in no way qualified to write this book," there is no irony here. Take the author at is word. Period. Ewk is truly not qualified. But the book still says something important. How do newbies see Zen? Ewk pretty much tells us. *They see Zen as a form of deviance. Zenists are untamable rebels who mock and hurl insults. Who speak non-sense. They reject Buddhism and could not care less about enlightenment.* Of course, not any of this is true. Zen is Chinese Buddhism with a heavy emphasis of mysticism: *to see our true nature becoming Buddhas*. The external forms of Zen such as meditation and koans are geared towards opening us up to direct gnosis of our unconditioned true nature which, I hasten to add, has many synonyms such as One Mind, Buddha-nature, pure Mind, unborn Mind, etc. If the newbie has issues with this, they are not on the right path. They still have a long ways to go. /r/zen will then be more like a biker's bar. A place of great tension with the male ego prepared for instant attack and defense.
